window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 — Introduction to Computer Systems","h":"","i":"csc-111-introduction-to-computer-systems","x":"CSC 111 — Introduction to Computer Systems Independent study guide This pack organizes the available course outline and lecture materials into a topic-by-topic study path. Search covers the text inside each note. Use the progress tracker to mark a topic complete only after you can explain it without looking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 — Introduction to Computer Systems","h":"Learning goals","i":"learning-goals","x":"Learning goals Work through each topic in order, or search for a specific term or example. Use the interactive recall checklist at the end of each note. Record confidence and revision dates in the progress tracker. Consult the source material when a note marks a gap or an equation needs checking."},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 — Introduction to Computer Systems","h":"Course notes","i":"course-notes","x":"Course notes 01 · Computer Systems: Foundations 02 · Computer Hardware Components 03 · Computer Software 04 · Windows and Operating Systems 05 · Internet and Email 06 · Word Processing 07 · Spreadsheets and Excel 08 · Presentations and Computer Networks"},{"u":"START_HERE.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 — Introduction to Computer Systems","h":"How to study","i":"how-to-study","x":"How to study Read one topic and mark its checklist as you complete each activity. Close the notes and write the key ideas from memory. Use search or “Find in note” to resolve specific gaps. Update your confidence and next review date in the progress tracker."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"","i":"your-study-plan","x":"Your study plan"},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Weekly study cycle","i":"weekly-study-cycle","x":"Weekly study cycle Preview the course outline and choose this week's topics. Study one or two notes per session. Do a closed-book recall after each note. Revisit weak topics within 48 hours. Complete a mixed-topic review at the end of the week."},{"u":"YOUR_STUDY_PLAN.html","g":"Start here","t":"Your study plan","h":"Exam-week cycle","i":"exam-week-cycle","x":"Exam-week cycle Review the progress tracker and prioritize low-confidence topics. Rework examples without looking at their solutions. Search the notes for definitions and distinctions you cannot recall. Complete the question bank and record errors in the tracker. Protect time for sleep and a final light review."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"","i":"csc-111-practical-review","x":"CSC 111 · Practical review Use this as a short final-pass checklist, then practise without looking at your notes. Follow the links for explanations and worked examples."},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"1 · Foundations","i":"1-foundations","x":"1 · Foundations Trace input → processing → output , with storage supporting each stage. Distinguish data from information, hardware from software, and capacity from speed. Binary place values (right to left): 1, 2, 4, 8, 16, 32. For 1101₂ , add 8 + 4 + 0 + 1 = 13₁₀ . Review generations by technology: vacuum tubes, transistors, integrated circuits, microprocessors, then modern VLSI/parallel development. Ask whether a result is accurate, useful, ethical, and based on trustworthy input. Open note 01"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"2 · Hardware","i":"2-hardware","x":"2 · Hardware Input enters data; CPU executes instructions; RAM holds current work; secondary storage keeps files; output presents results. CPU = control unit + arithmetic/logic unit + registers. RAM is volatile; ROM/firmware is persistent; cache is very fast. OCR recognizes text; OMR detects marked boxes. A touchscreen is both input and output. Protect devices from heat, liquids, unsafe power, static, and unauthorized access. Open note 02"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"3 · Software","i":"3-software","x":"3 · Software System software manages the computer; applications help users complete tasks. OS, driver, utility, and application are different roles. Compiler, interpreter, and assembler translate code differently. Algorithm: define input, process, output, test with a sample case. Malware: virus, worm, Trojan, ransomware, spyware. Keep software updated, back up, and report suspicious activity. Open note 03"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"4 · Windows and files","i":"4-windows-and-files","x":"4 · Windows and files Startup: firmware checks hardware → boot device/loader → OS loads → sign-in. Create folder → use a clear filename → Save As to the correct location → Save changes → verify destination. Copy preserves original; move relocates it; shortcut points to it. Do not change extensions to convert files. Lock when stepping away; sign out to end a session; restart to reload Windows. Save first. Open note 04"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"5 · Internet and email","i":"5-internet-and-email","x":"5 · Internet and email Internet = interconnected networks; Web = a service on it; browser displays pages; search engine helps find them. Evaluate author, evidence, date, purpose, and citations. Compare sources and cite what you use. Email: verify recipient, use specific subject, concise message, correct attachment; proofread before send. Cc is visible to recipients; Bcc hides addresses from one another. Verify unusual requests through a separate channel. Open note 05"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"6 · Word","i":"6-word","x":"6 · Word Save As first; save regularly. Use heading styles, consistent paragraph formatting, and page breaks. Insert tables/images from the ribbon; preserve image proportions; add page numbers where needed. Review spelling and grammar, then proofread names/numbers and inspect print preview. Mail merge combines a template and recipient list: clean data → connect → insert fields → preview → finish carefully. Open note 06"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"7 · Excel","i":"7-excel","x":"7 · Excel Cell B3 means column B, row 3. Range B2:B6 includes both endpoints. Formula starts with = : =B2*C2 . Common functions: SUM , AVERAGE , MIN , MAX , COUNT . Relative B1 changes when copied; absolute $B$1 remains fixed. Sort complete records; filter to inspect; select labels and values before inserting a chart. Check formula bar and errors; save .xlsx to preserve workbook features. Open note 07"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"8 · PowerPoint and networks","i":"8-powerpoint-and-networks","x":"8 · PowerPoint and networks Slides should communicate one main point each; keep text readable, use consistent design, cite sources, rehearse. Network benefits: communication/resource sharing. Risks: access abuse, privacy loss, malware, outages. LAN = local site; WAN = wider area. Switch connects within a LAN; router directs traffic between networks. Use approved Wi-Fi, unique passwords, updates, and restrained sharing. Open note 08"},{"u":"CHEAT_SHEET.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Practical review","h":"Final practice routine","i":"final-practice-routine","x":"Final practice routine Choose one question from each topic in the question bank . Attempt it closed-book; show calculations and procedures, not just the final result. Compare with the worked answer key , correct in a different color, and log a next action in the progress tracker ."},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Progress tracker","h":"","i":"csc-111-progress-tracker","x":"CSC 111 · Progress tracker Record a score after answering the linked questions without notes. Suggested confidence scale: 1 = not yet, 3 = partly secure, 5 = can explain and apply. Fill each blank with your own result and next action. Topic Note Question IDs Score /3 Confidence /5 Review action Foundations Note 01 Q01–Q03 Hardware Note 02 Q04–Q06 Software Note 03 Q07–Q09 Windows and files Note 04 Q10–Q12 Internet and email Note 05 Q13–Q15 Word processing Note 06 Q16–Q18 Excel Note 07 Q19–Q21 Presentations and networks Note 08 Q22–Q24"},{"u":"PROGRESS_TRACKER.html","g":"Start here","t":"CSC 111 · Progress tracker","h":"Review routine","i":"review-routine","x":"Review routine Attempt all three questions for a topic closed-book; award one point for each substantially correct response. Compare with the worked answers and write a specific correction or practice action in the last column. Revisit low-confidence topics within a few days; answer missed questions again without copying the key. Keep names, passwords, personal details, and other sensitive information out of this tracker."},{"u":"notes/01_computer-systems-foundations.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations","h":"","i":"01-computer-systems-foundations","x":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations"},{"u":"notes/01_computer-systems-foundations.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations","h":"What a computer does","i":"what-a-computer-does","x":"What a computer does A computer is an electronic, programmable system that accepts data and instructions, processes them, stores them, and produces information. Its basic cycle is input → processing → output , with storage available throughout. Data are raw facts (such as 18, 21, 19 ); information is data organized to be useful (such as an average age of 19.3). Hardware is the physical equipment; software is the instructions that direct it. Neither is useful alone: an application needs hardware to run, and hardware needs software to perform useful tasks. Computers are fast, consistent at repeated work, accurate when data and instructions are correct, able to store large amounts, and useful for many different tasks. They do not understand meaning or make dependable judgments by themselves. Incorrect data or instructions produce incorrect results (often summarized as garbage in, garbage out ); computers also need power, maintenance, and human oversight."},{"u":"notes/01_computer-systems-foundations.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations","h":"Development and types","i":"development-and-types","x":"Development and types Computing developed from tools such as the abacus and mechanical calculators to programmable machines. Charles Babbage proposed the Analytical Engine; later electronic generations became smaller, faster, more reliable, and more affordable: Generation Main technology Broad change First Vacuum tubes Very large, hot machines; machine-language programming Second Transistors Smaller, faster, less heat; wider use of high-level languages Third Integrated circuits More components per chip; improved reliability and capacity Fourth Microprocessors CPU on a chip; personal computers became practical Fifth / current development VLSI, parallel processing, AI research Greater integration and increasingly capable systems These are broad historical groupings, not a claim that every computer changed at once. Computers are also grouped by purpose (general-purpose or special-purpose), portability (desktop, laptop, tablet, embedded), and capability (for example, servers and supercomputers)."},{"u":"notes/01_computer-systems-foundations.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations","h":"Number systems you should be able to work","i":"number-systems-you-should-be-able-to-work","x":"Number systems you should be able to work Digital circuits represent information with two states, conventionally 0 and 1 . A bit is one binary digit; a byte is 8 bits. Decimal uses base 10; binary uses base 2; hexadecimal uses base 16 (digits 0–9 and A–F). Convert binary 1011₂ to decimal: label places from right to left as 1, 2, 4, 8. Add the places with a 1: 8 + 0 + 2 + 1 = 11₁₀ . Convert decimal 13 to binary: repeatedly divide by 2 and record remainders: 13→6 r1 , 6→3 r0 , 3→1 r1 , 1→0 r1 . Read remainders upward: 1101₂ . Storage units are commonly written KB, MB, GB, TB. In binary-based teaching examples, 1 KB = 1,024 bytes; some modern storage labels use decimal multiples (1,000). State which convention you use if precision matters."},{"u":"notes/01_computer-systems-foundations.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations","h":"Safety, ethics, and study","i":"safety-ethics-and-study","x":"Safety, ethics, and study Use devices with good posture and lighting, take breaks, keep liquids away, protect equipment from heat and power surges, and shut down safely. Respect privacy, intellectual property, accessibility, and other users; verify information before relying on or sharing it. Use technology lawfully and credit sources. Common mistakes A computer processes data; it does not guarantee that the result is true or sensible. Do not confuse hardware (touchable components) with software (programs and data). When converting binary, write place values first and read division remainders from bottom to top."},{"u":"notes/01_computer-systems-foundations.html","g":"Notes","t":"01 · Computer Systems: Foundations","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Explain the input–process–output cycle using an example that includes stored data. Name one defining technology for each of the first four computer generations. Convert 10010₂ to decimal, then explain why a correct calculation can still produce a misleading result. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"","i":"02-computer-hardware-components","x":"02 · Computer Hardware Components"},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"Main hardware units","i":"main-hardware-units","x":"Main hardware units The computer’s hardware works together through five functional groups: Input devices enter data and commands: keyboard, mouse, touchscreen, microphone, scanner, camera, barcode reader, OCR (recognizes printed characters), and OMR (detects marked responses). The CPU (processor) executes instructions. The control unit (CU) coordinates activity; the arithmetic/logic unit (ALU) performs calculations and comparisons; registers hold tiny amounts of data needed immediately. Primary memory holds instructions and data currently in use. RAM is fast, volatile working memory; its contents normally disappear when power is removed. ROM/firmware retains essential startup instructions. Cache is very fast memory that keeps frequently needed data close to the CPU. Secondary storage keeps files when power is off: SSDs, hard drives, USB flash drives, memory cards, and optical discs. SSDs have no moving parts; hard drives use magnetic rotating media. Back up important work to a separate, trusted location. Output devices present results: monitor, printer, speakers/headphones, and projector. Printers may be impact (strike a ribbon, e.g. dot-matrix) or non-impact (e.g. inkjet, laser);"},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"Main hardware units","i":"main-hardware-units","x":"ent results: monitor, printer, speakers/headphones, and projector. Printers may be impact (strike a ribbon, e.g. dot-matrix) or non-impact (e.g. inkjet, laser); impact models are typically noisier. Some peripherals both receive and present information. A touchscreen is both input and output; a network adapter sends and receives data."},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"What happens when you run a task?","i":"what-happens-when-you-run-a-task","x":"What happens when you run a task? When you calculate a total, the keyboard or mouse supplies input, the program and needed data are loaded from storage into RAM, the CPU fetches and executes instructions, and the result appears on screen or is saved. The CPU works on instructions; storage preserves data. This is why a machine can have ample disk space but still feel slow when RAM is insufficient."},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"Practical distinctions","i":"practical-distinctions","x":"Practical distinctions RAM vs. storage: RAM is temporary working space; storage is long-term, non-volatile space. Input vs. output: a scanner captures a paper page; a printer produces a paper copy. OCR vs. OMR: OCR turns image text into editable characters; OMR detects filled marks on a designed form. Capacity vs. speed: capacity is how much can be kept; speed is how quickly it can be read or written."},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"A safe peripheral workflow","i":"a-safe-peripheral-workflow","x":"A safe peripheral workflow Check that the device and cable/port are compatible; never force a connector. Connect or pair it as directed, then allow the operating system to install or select a trusted driver. Test with a small, non-sensitive file (for example, print one page). Save or eject removable media safely before unplugging it. Do not remove a drive while it is writing."},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"Safety and care","i":"safety-and-care","x":"Safety and care Keep vents unobstructed; avoid static discharge when handling internal parts; use dry hands and appropriate power protection; never open a powered device. Keep food and liquids away. Use headphones at a safe volume and adjust screen, chair, and keyboard to reduce strain. Recycle batteries and electronic waste through approved channels. Ask for authorization before inspecting another person’s files or connecting equipment to a managed network. Common mistakes RAM is not the same as a hard drive or SSD; one is working memory and the other is persistent storage. The CPU does not permanently store all your documents. A scanner is input; a monitor and projector are output. A multifunction device may perform both."},{"u":"notes/02_computer-hardware-components.html","g":"Notes","t":"02 · Computer Hardware Components","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Trace the path of a typed number through input, memory, CPU, and output. Compare RAM, ROM, cache, and secondary storage by purpose and persistence. Choose an input and output device for digitizing a printed questionnaire and explain why. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"","i":"03-computer-software","x":"03 · Computer Software"},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"Categories and roles","i":"categories-and-roles","x":"Categories and roles Software is a program or related set of instructions and data. System software operates and manages the computer; application software helps a user complete a task. Type Examples and purpose Operating system Windows or Linux manages memory, files, devices, accounts, and running programs Device driver Lets the OS communicate with a particular printer, display, or other device Utility Security scanning, backup, compression, file management, and maintenance Language translator Compiler translates a program before execution; interpreter translates/executes stepwise; assembler translates assembly language Application Word processor, spreadsheet, browser, presentation tool, or database manager Applications may be general-purpose (used for many tasks) or special-purpose (built for a particular activity). Off-the-shelf software is ready-made; custom software is developed for a specific user or organization. Choose software that is legitimate, supported, suitable for the task, and compatible with the device. A database is an organized collection of related data. A DBMS (database management system) creates, searches, updates, and controls access to databases. A"},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"Categories and roles","i":"categories-and-roles","x":"vice. A database is an organized collection of related data. A DBMS (database management system) creates, searches, updates, and controls access to databases. A spreadsheet is convenient for calculations and small tables; a DBMS is better for structured, related records and controlled multi-user access."},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"Programs and simple algorithms","i":"programs-and-simple-algorithms","x":"Programs and simple algorithms A program is an ordered set of instructions. An algorithm is a finite, clear procedure for solving a problem; pseudocode describes its logic in readable steps. A good algorithm states inputs, processing, outputs, and a stopping condition. Example: calculate a student’s mean from three marks. INPUT mark1, mark2, mark3 total = mark1 + mark2 + mark3 mean = total / 3 OUTPUT mean To check the logic, try 60, 75, 90 : total 225 , mean 75 . A programming language expresses such steps in a form a computer can translate and execute. Testing checks expected cases; debugging finds and corrects defects."},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"Malware and safe computing","i":"malware-and-safe-computing","x":"Malware and safe computing Malware is software intended to harm, disrupt, spy on, or gain unauthorized access to systems. A virus attaches to files and spreads when they run; a worm spreads across systems; a Trojan disguises itself as useful software; ransomware locks or encrypts data to demand payment; spyware collects information covertly. Antivirus tools, firewalls, updates, least-privilege accounts, and offline or separate backups reduce risk but do not replace careful behavior."},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"Safe response procedure","i":"safe-response-procedure","x":"Safe response procedure Do not open unexpected attachments, enable unknown macros, or install software from untrusted sources. If a device behaves suspiciously, stop entering passwords or sensitive data and disconnect it from the network if safe and permitted. Tell the responsible support person; follow institutional incident procedures rather than deleting evidence or forwarding malware. Restore files only from known-good backups and change exposed passwords from a clean device. Use licensed software, respect copyright and privacy, protect passwords, and do not install or run programs on devices without permission. Common mistakes An operating system is system software; a spreadsheet is application software. A compiler and interpreter both translate code, but they do so differently. Antivirus cannot guarantee safety. Updates, backups, caution, and reporting still matter."},{"u":"notes/03_computer-software.html","g":"Notes","t":"03 · Computer Software","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Classify a printer driver, spreadsheet, operating system, and backup utility. Write pseudocode to convert minutes to hours and remaining minutes; test it with 135 minutes. Distinguish a virus, worm, Trojan, and ransomware, then give a safe first response to a suspicious attachment. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/04_windows-and-operating-systems.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems","h":"","i":"04-windows-and-operating-systems","x":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems"},{"u":"notes/04_windows-and-operating-systems.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems","h":"What the OS does","i":"what-the-os-does","x":"What the OS does An operating system (OS) is system software that provides the user interface and coordinates programs, CPU time, memory, files, storage, and peripheral devices. Windows presents a graphical interface with windows, menus, icons, and a pointer. Common desktop elements include the Start menu, taskbar, notification area, File Explorer, and Recycle Bin. When a computer starts, firmware performs initial checks (POST), finds a boot device, loads the OS, and presents the sign-in screen. A cold boot starts from power off; a restart/warm boot reloads the system without a full power-off. Save work before either."},{"u":"notes/04_windows-and-operating-systems.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems","h":"File and folder procedure","i":"file-and-folder-procedure","x":"File and folder procedure Use File Explorer to organize work rather than leaving files scattered on the desktop. Open File Explorer and choose a suitable location, such as Documents or an approved course folder. Select New folder , give it a clear name (for example, CSC111_Revision ), and press Enter. Open that folder; create descriptive filenames such as network-notes.docx . Save a new document with Save As to choose its location and format. Use Save for later changes. To move a file, select it and use Cut then Paste at the destination; use Copy when you need to keep the original too. Check the destination and filename before deleting anything. Deleted files may be recoverable from the Recycle Bin until it is emptied. A path identifies a file’s location, such as Documents\\CSC111\\week1.docx . A file extension (such as .docx or .xlsx ) indicates the file type; changing the visible name alone does not reliably convert the format. A shortcut points to an item; it is not a second copy of the file."},{"u":"notes/04_windows-and-operating-systems.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems","h":"Find and manage a file","i":"find-and-manage-a-file","x":"Find and manage a file Search by a distinctive filename or part of it; check the current folder and filters if no result appears. Rename using a clear, descriptive name; avoid changing the extension. Back up important files to an approved separate location. Cloud synchronization is useful but is not the same as an independent backup. Use Lock when stepping away briefly; Sign out ends your session; Restart reloads Windows. Choose the option appropriate to the situation."},{"u":"notes/04_windows-and-operating-systems.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems","h":"Accounts, privacy, and maintenance","i":"accounts-privacy-and-maintenance","x":"Accounts, privacy, and maintenance Use your own account, a strong unique password, and a screen lock. Do not share credentials or store them in plain text. Install updates and applications only through trusted, authorized channels. Check permissions before sharing folders. Follow school/work policy for removable drives and cloud storage. Before disposing of a device, have its data securely erased by an authorized process. Maintain good posture, keep vents clear, protect devices from liquid and heat, and shut down through the operating system. Forced power-off can lose unsaved work or damage files; use it only when normal controls have failed. Common mistakes A shortcut is a link to a file or program, not the file itself. Save As changes the destination/name/format; Save updates the current document. Moving a file differs from copying it. Verify the destination before removing the source. Do not empty the Recycle Bin until you are sure you no longer need its contents."},{"u":"notes/04_windows-and-operating-systems.html","g":"Notes","t":"04 · Windows and Operating Systems","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall List the stages from pressing the power button to the Windows sign-in screen. Describe how to create a course folder, save a document into it, and verify the saved file. Compare Lock, Sign out, Restart, and Shut down; give one appropriate use for each. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"","i":"05-internet-and-email","x":"05 · Internet and Email"},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"Web and research","i":"web-and-research","x":"Web and research The Internet is a global network of interconnected networks using agreed protocols. The World Wide Web is one service on the Internet: linked pages and resources accessed with a browser. A browser displays web content; a search engine indexes content and helps find it. A URL identifies a resource; DNS helps translate a domain name into a network address. An ISP provides Internet access; a modem/router connects a home or organization to its service."},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"Search and evaluate information","i":"search-and-evaluate-information","x":"Search and evaluate information Turn the question into two or three distinctive search terms; use quotation marks for an exact phrase when useful. Open results that appear relevant, not just the first one. Compare independent sources. Check author or organization, evidence, publication date, purpose, and whether claims are supported. Follow citations to original sources where possible; distinguish evidence from opinion or advertising. Record enough bibliographic information to cite the source. Respect copyright, licenses, and privacy; do not present another person’s work as your own. Avoid entering passwords or personal details on an unexpected page. Check the domain and secure connection, but remember that a padlock does not prove a site is honest. Keep software updated and use reputable sources."},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"Email essentials","i":"email-essentials","x":"Email essentials An email address is usually mailbox@domain . A message has recipients, subject, body, and may have attachments. To is for primary recipients; Cc shares a copy; Bcc hides recipients’ addresses from one another. A mailbox commonly includes Inbox, Drafts, Sent, Spam/Junk, and Trash."},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"Compose and send a clear message","i":"compose-and-send-a-clear-message","x":"Compose and send a clear message Select Compose/New message and enter the recipient address carefully. Use Cc only when those recipients need visibility; use Bcc to protect a group’s addresses when appropriate. Write a specific subject and a concise, courteous message with necessary context and a clear request. Attach the correct file, verify its name and version, and ensure it contains no unintended personal information. Recheck recipients, subject, wording, and attachment before sending. Use professional netiquette: greet the recipient, avoid all-caps or hostile language, proofread, and reply only to people who need the response. Do not forward private messages without permission. Treat messages asking for urgent payment, passwords, or unexpected attachments as suspicious. Verify unusual requests through a separate trusted channel; report phishing rather than replying or clicking. Common mistakes The Web is a service that uses the Internet; the terms are not interchangeable. Cc recipients are visible; Bcc is for hiding addresses from other recipients, not for evading policy. A familiar display name does not authenticate a sender. Inspect the actual address and verify unusual"},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"Compose and send a clear message","i":"compose-and-send-a-clear-message","x":"g addresses from other recipients, not for evading policy. A familiar display name does not authenticate a sender. Inspect the actual address and verify unusual requests. Search results are not automatically accurate or unbiased; evaluate and cite sources."},{"u":"notes/05_internet-and-email.html","g":"Notes","t":"05 · Internet and Email","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Explain the difference between Internet, Web, browser, and search engine. For a class email to 25 students, when might Bcc be preferable to Cc? Explain the privacy reason. Give a four-step method for judging whether an online source is trustworthy and suitable for an assignment. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"","i":"06-word-processing","x":"06 · Word Processing"},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Build a clean document","i":"build-a-clean-document","x":"Build a clean document A word processor creates, edits, formats, saves, and prints text documents. In Word, the ribbon groups commands (for example, Home, Insert, Layout, References, Review). Start with a clear purpose and structure before decorating the page."},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Create, save, and format","i":"create-save-and-format","x":"Create, save, and format Open Word and choose Blank document or an approved template. Type or paste your content; use Save As early to choose the course folder, a descriptive filename, and .docx . Save regularly with Ctrl+S. Organize content with built-in Heading 1/Heading 2 styles and Normal for body text. Use a consistent, readable font and size. Set page size, orientation, and margins from Layout . Use paragraph spacing and line spacing consistently; use alignment and indentation intentionally. Use numbered or bulleted lists for genuine sequences or groups. For page layout, use page breaks rather than repeated blank lines. Add tables from Insert → Table when information is naturally tabular. Add meaningful headers; keep tables simple and readable. Insert a picture from Insert → Pictures , resize using a corner handle to preserve proportions, set suitable text wrapping, and add alt text if available. Use only images you may lawfully use and cite them when required. Add headers, footers, and page numbers from Insert when helpful. Use References tools for a table of contents, citations, or footnotes. Styles apply consistent formatting and structure; manual formatting changes only"},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Create, save, and format","i":"create-save-and-format","x":"helpful. Use References tools for a table of contents, citations, or footnotes. Styles apply consistent formatting and structure; manual formatting changes only the selected text. Headings based on styles can be used to generate a table of contents."},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Review and submit","i":"review-and-submit","x":"Review and submit Run spelling and grammar checks from Review ; accept suggestions only when they preserve your intended meaning. Use Find/Replace carefully. Review each replacement where context matters. Use File → Print to inspect print preview, page count, margins, page breaks, and grayscale/readability. Save, reopen the saved copy if practical, and confirm the filename and destination before submitting."},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Mail merge in brief","i":"mail-merge-in-brief","x":"Mail merge in brief Mail merge combines a template (for example, a letter) with a recipient data source (for example, a spreadsheet). Prepare clean column headings and one person per row, select Mailings → Start Mail Merge , connect the list, insert fields such as Name, preview several records, correct errors, and finish/print only the intended set. Protect recipient data and do not send a test to the full list."},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Common pitfalls and good practice","i":"common-pitfalls-and-good-practice","x":"Common pitfalls and good practice Use one space after sentences; avoid aligning text with repeated spaces. Do not press Enter repeatedly to force a new page. Check that a heading is a real heading style, not merely large bold text. Keep a backup before major edits; use Track Changes or comments when collaborative review is required. Proofread names, dates, page numbers, citations, and confidential content. Use accessible contrast, descriptive link text, and alt text. Follow the assignment’s file format and naming rules. Common mistakes Ctrl+S saves the current file; it does not create a separately named copy. Use Save As for a new copy. Formatting text to look like a heading is not the same as applying a heading style. Spell-check may miss real-word errors (for example, “form” instead of “from”); proofread manually. Check print preview: on-screen layout can hide page breaks, clipped tables, or missing content."},{"u":"notes/06_word-processing.html","g":"Notes","t":"06 · Word Processing","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Give the steps to create a styled report with a title, section headings, page numbers, and a saved .docx copy. Explain why page breaks and heading styles are better than repeated blank lines and manual bolding. Outline a safe mail-merge workflow and identify two checks to make before sending. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/07_spreadsheets-and-excel.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","h":"","i":"07-spreadsheets-and-excel","x":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel"},{"u":"notes/07_spreadsheets-and-excel.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","h":"Workbook structure and data","i":"workbook-structure-and-data","x":"Workbook structure and data An Excel workbook is a file containing one or more worksheets . Rows are numbered; columns are lettered. Their intersection is a cell , addressed by column then row (for example, B3 ). A range is a group of cells (for example, B2:B6 ). The active cell is where typed data or a formula will go. Text labels describe data; numbers, dates, and times can be values used in calculations. Format controls how a value appears, not its underlying value. Use one header row, one record per row, one variable per column, and avoid blank rows inside a data table."},{"u":"notes/07_spreadsheets-and-excel.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","h":"Enter and calculate","i":"enter-and-calculate","x":"Enter and calculate Open a blank workbook and save it as .xlsx in the right folder. Enter descriptive headings (for example, Item , Quantity , Unit price , Total ) and data beneath the matching columns. Click the result cell and begin every formula with = . Use cell references rather than retyping values. For example, if quantity is B2 and unit price is C2 , enter =B2*C2 in D2 . Press Enter; inspect the result and formula bar. Fill the formula down when appropriate, then spot-check a row. Common functions: =SUM(D2:D6) adds values; =AVERAGE(D2:D6) returns their mean; =MIN(D2:D6) and =MAX(D2:D6) find extremes; =COUNT(D2:D6) counts numeric cells. Example: totals 12, 15, 18 yield SUM = 45 , AVERAGE = 15 . Relative references adjust when copied ( B2 becomes B3 ). Absolute references stay fixed ( $B$1 ), useful for a constant tax rate: if E2 is subtotal and $B$1 is the rate, use =E2*$B$1 . Mixed references lock only a row or column (e.g. $B2 , B$2 )."},{"u":"notes/07_spreadsheets-and-excel.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","h":"Make data useful","i":"make-data-useful","x":"Make data useful Format numbers as currency, percentages, or dates to match their meaning; do not type a currency symbol into every numeric value. Sort the entire table by a chosen field, keeping headers identified. Filter to show matching rows without deleting the others. Create a chart by selecting the relevant labels and values, then Insert → Chart . Choose a chart that fits the comparison or trend; include a meaningful title and labeled axes where useful. Use print preview, set orientation/scaling if needed, and confirm that all columns and headings are visible. Save as .xlsx to retain formulas and formatting. CSV stores plain tabular text and generally does not preserve multiple sheets, formatting, or formulas as formulas."},{"u":"notes/07_spreadsheets-and-excel.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","h":"Troubleshooting and care","i":"troubleshooting-and-care","x":"Troubleshooting and care #### often means a column is too narrow; widen it. #DIV/0! means division by zero or a blank denominator. #VALUE! often indicates an unexpected type; #NAME? may mean a misspelled function or missing text quotes. A result that does not update may be text rather than a formula or may use incorrect calculation settings. Protect original data before sorting, deleting, or overwriting; keep a copy and use Undo when appropriate. Do not expose personal data in shared workbooks. Use clear labels, appropriate precision, and honest chart scales—visualizations should not mislead. Common mistakes A formula starts with = ; typing B2*C2 without it displays text instead of calculating. =SUM(B2:B6) includes both endpoints; a comma-separated list such as =SUM(B2,B6) is different. A number format changes display, not the stored value. Check formulas and references, not only what appears on screen. Sort whole records, not one column alone, or values can become associated with the wrong person/item."},{"u":"notes/07_spreadsheets-and-excel.html","g":"Notes","t":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall In B2:B6 , enter 8, 12, 10, 15, 5. Write formulas for their total and average and calculate each result. Explain the difference between B1 and $B$1 when a formula is copied down. Describe how to sort/filter a table and make a chart without separating data from its labels. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"notes/08_presentations-and-computer-networks.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks","h":"","i":"08-presentations-and-computer-networks","x":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks"},{"u":"notes/08_presentations-and-computer-networks.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks","h":"PowerPoint: prepare and deliver","i":"powerpoint-prepare-and-deliver","x":"PowerPoint: prepare and deliver A presentation communicates ideas to an audience; a slide is one page in the deck. Plan a beginning, a few logically ordered sections, and a conclusion before adding effects."},{"u":"notes/08_presentations-and-computer-networks.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks","h":"Create a usable slide deck","i":"create-a-usable-slide-deck","x":"Create a usable slide deck Open a blank presentation or suitable template. Save early as .pptx with a clear filename. Use a title slide, then add slides with Home → New Slide and choose a layout that matches each slide’s content. Give each slide one main point. Use short phrases, readable text, consistent fonts/colors, and sufficient contrast. Insert an image, shape, chart, or SmartArt from Insert only when it clarifies the message. Check image permission, source, quality, and alt text. Use Slide Sorter to inspect order and Slide Show to rehearse. Check timings, spelling, charts, transitions, and whether media works on the presentation device. Keep animation and transitions restrained; use them to direct attention, not as decoration. Save again and carry a backup copy using an approved method. Use slide notes for speaking prompts rather than crowding the slide. Cite sources for facts, data, and images. Do not put sensitive information on a projected or shared slide."},{"u":"notes/08_presentations-and-computer-networks.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks","h":"Network essentials","i":"network-essentials","x":"Network essentials A computer network connects devices so they can exchange data and share services. A network node is a connected device. Common scales are PAN (personal), LAN (local site), MAN (city/metro), and WAN (large geographic area). The Internet is a global network of networks. Network types may be client–server (central servers provide services to clients) or peer-to-peer (devices share directly). Common topologies include star (devices connect through a central switch/access point), bus (shared backbone), and ring (devices form a loop). Each has different cost, performance, and failure characteristics. Component Role NIC / wireless adapter Connects a device to a network Switch Connects devices within a LAN and forwards local traffic Router Connects networks and directs traffic between them Modem Converts signals for a particular access link/service Access point Provides wireless access to a wired network Bandwidth is the capacity of a link; throughput is the rate actually achieved. Protocols are agreed communication rules: IP addresses devices/routes packets; TCP helps deliver data reliably; DNS resolves domain names; HTTP/HTTPS carries web traffic (HTTPS encrypts the"},{"u":"notes/08_presentations-and-computer-networks.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks","h":"Network essentials","i":"network-essentials","x":"ation rules: IP addresses devices/routes packets; TCP helps deliver data reliably; DNS resolves domain names; HTTP/HTTPS carries web traffic (HTTPS encrypts the connection to the site). Networks enable communication and sharing printers, files, applications, and Internet access. Risks include outages, unauthorized access, malware, privacy loss, and congestion. Use approved networks, strong unique passwords, multi-factor authentication when offered, updates, and encrypted connections. Do not join unfamiliar Wi-Fi for sensitive work; avoid sharing personal data publicly. A network connection does not itself make shared data trustworthy or backed up. Common mistakes A LAN describes a limited area; a WAN spans a wider area. The Internet is not the same thing as Wi-Fi. A switch connects devices within a network; a router joins/directs traffic between networks. In PowerPoint, animation affects objects on a slide; a transition is the change between slides. More effects do not automatically improve a presentation. Clarity, rehearsal, and accessible design matter more."},{"u":"notes/08_presentations-and-computer-networks.html","g":"Notes","t":"08 · Presentations and Computer Networks","h":"Active recall","i":"active-recall","x":"Active recall Design a four-slide outline for explaining one course concept; state the purpose of each slide. Compare LAN, WAN, and the Internet, then distinguish a switch from a router. Name two network benefits and two risks, and give a safe practice for each risk. Practise this topic: Question bank"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"","i":"csc-111-specific-question-bank","x":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank Attempt each question before opening the worked answer key . Show your reasoning, steps, and checks. IDs are used in the progress tracker."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"01 · Foundations","i":"01-foundations","x":"01 · Foundations Q01. A student types three quiz scores, calculates their mean, and saves it. Identify input, processing, output, and storage in this example. Q02. Convert 10110₂ to decimal. Show the place values and addition. Q03. Compare first-generation and fourth-generation computers by their principal technology and one practical consequence of the change."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"02 · Hardware","i":"02-hardware","x":"02 · Hardware Q04. A user is editing a report, has 8 GB of free disk space, but many programs are open and the computer is slow. Explain the difference between RAM and storage and identify a likely resource bottleneck. Q05. For scanning a printed multiple-choice answer sheet, choose OCR or OMR and justify the choice. What would be suitable for converting a paragraph on the page into editable text? Q06. Trace how a key press becomes a calculated result shown on screen. Name the major hardware units in order."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"03 · Software","i":"03-software","x":"03 · Software Q07. Classify each item as system software or application software: Windows, a printer driver, a backup utility, and Excel. Explain each classification. Q08. Write pseudocode to convert a temperature in Celsius to Fahrenheit using F = C × 9/5 + 32 . Test it with C = 20 . Q09. A message claims to be an invoice, urges immediate action, and includes an unexpected attachment. Identify two warning signs and give a safe response sequence."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"04 · Windows and files","i":"04-windows-and-files","x":"04 · Windows and files Q10. Give the steps to create CSC111\\Week1 in Documents, save review.docx there, and verify it was saved in the right place. Q11. Explain the difference between copying, moving, and creating a shortcut to a file. Which method preserves the original and creates a separate file copy? Q12. Your computer is unresponsive but the sign-in screen is available. Compare Lock, Sign out, and Restart; choose the appropriate action if you are stepping away briefly."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"05 · Internet and email","i":"05-internet-and-email","x":"05 · Internet and email Q13. Explain how the Internet, the Web, a browser, and a search engine relate to one another. Q14. You need a reliable, current source for a health statistic. List four checks you would make before using and citing a search result. Q15. You are emailing a course announcement to 30 students and do not want them to see one another’s addresses. Which recipient field should you use, and what checks should you complete before sending?"},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"06 · Word processing","i":"06-word-processing","x":"06 · Word processing Q16. Give a procedure for creating a two-section report with a title, consistent headings, page numbers, and a saved .docx file. Q17. Why are heading styles and page breaks preferable to manually bolding headings and pressing Enter repeatedly? Give two reasons. Q18. Outline the steps and two safety checks for a mail merge that sends personalized letters from a spreadsheet list."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","i":"07-spreadsheets-and-excel","x":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel Q19. Cells B2:B5 contain 12, 18, 15, and 5. Give Excel formulas for the sum and average, and calculate both. Q20. Cell B1 contains a tax rate of 0.16 and C2 contains a price of 250. Write a formula in D2 to calculate tax using the rate in B1 , then give the formula after it is copied to D3 . Q21. You sort a table by surname but only the surname column moves. Explain the resulting problem and the correct way to sort. Name one useful chart for comparing sales across four products."},{"u":"practice/QUESTION_BANK.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Specific question bank","h":"08 · Presentations and networks","i":"08-presentations-and-networks","x":"08 · Presentations and networks Q22. Plan a four-slide presentation on computer safety: give each slide a purpose and one design choice that improves clarity. Q23. A campus office network connects computers, a shared printer, and an Internet gateway. Identify the network scale/type, explain the roles of a switch and router, and state one benefit. Q24. Give two benefits and two risks of networks, then pair each risk with a practical safe-use measure."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"","i":"csc-111-worked-answer-key","x":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key Use these as model reasoning, not text to memorize. Equivalent wording is acceptable when the concepts and steps are correct."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"01 · Foundations","i":"01-foundations","x":"01 · Foundations Q01. Input is the three typed scores and the instruction to calculate a mean. Processing adds the values and divides by three. Output is the displayed mean. Storage is the saved file or record that retains the result after the program closes. Q02. From right to left, the place values are 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. Thus 10110₂ = 1×16 + 0×8 + 1×4 + 1×2 + 0×1 = 22₁₀ . Q03. First-generation computers used vacuum tubes; they were large, hot, power-hungry, and required considerable maintenance. Fourth-generation systems use microprocessors, putting the CPU on a chip; this enabled smaller, cheaper personal computers with much greater capability."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"02 · Hardware","i":"02-hardware","x":"02 · Hardware Q04. RAM is temporary, fast working memory used by running programs; disk/SSD storage keeps files persistently. Free disk space does not indicate available RAM. Many open programs may exhaust working memory and cause slowdowns, so insufficient RAM is a likely bottleneck (though CPU or other causes are also possible). Q05. Use OMR for a carefully designed answer sheet with filled marks. Use OCR to recognize the printed paragraph and turn it into editable characters. Q06. Keyboard (input) sends the key data to the system; data/instructions are held in RAM; CPU control and arithmetic/logic units execute the calculation; the display (output) shows the result. If the result is saved, secondary storage retains it."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"03 · Software","i":"03-software","x":"03 · Software Q07. Windows is an OS, therefore system software. A printer driver is system software that lets the OS control a device. A backup utility is system software/utility software that supports maintenance. Excel is application software for spreadsheet tasks. Q08. INPUT C F = C * 9 / 5 + 32 OUTPUT F For C = 20 : 20 × 9 / 5 + 32 = 36 + 32 = 68°F . Include parentheses if the chosen language’s operator precedence requires them: (C * 9 / 5) + 32 . Q09. Warning signs include an unexpected attachment and pressure to act immediately; sender/address mismatch or a request for credentials/payment are additional signs. Do not open or reply; verify with the supposed sender through a separate trusted channel, then report/delete according to policy. If opened, notify support promptly and follow incident instructions."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"04 · Windows and files","i":"04-windows-and-files","x":"04 · Windows and files Q10. Open File Explorer → Documents → New folder → name it CSC111 → open it → create Week1 → open that folder. In Word choose Save As, browse to Documents\\CSC111\\Week1 , enter review.docx , and save. Verify the title/filename and location in the Save dialog or File Explorer; reopening the file is a useful check. Q11. Copy creates a separate file while retaining the original. Move relocates the item to another location. A shortcut points to the original and is not an independent copy. Copy is the method that preserves the original and creates another file. Q12. Lock protects the current signed-in session and leaves programs running; sign out closes the user session and programs; restart closes and reloads the OS. For a brief absence, Lock is appropriate."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"05 · Internet and email","i":"05-internet-and-email","x":"05 · Internet and email Q13. The Internet is the global system of connected networks. The Web is a service running over it, made of linked pages and resources. A browser requests and displays Web content. A search engine indexes content and helps locate pages; it is accessed using a browser. Q14. Check (1) who authored/published it and their relevant expertise, (2) evidence and links to original data, (3) date and whether it is current, (4) purpose, funding, or possible bias. Compare independent authoritative sources and cite the source actually consulted. Do not treat high search ranking as proof of reliability. Q15. Use Bcc to conceal recipients’ addresses from one another (or use an approved mailing-list service). Before sending, verify the address/list, subject and message, attachment and its privacy, and that Bcc is populated correctly; send a test to yourself if appropriate."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"06 · Word processing","i":"06-word-processing","x":"06 · Word processing Q16. Create a blank document and Save As .docx in the correct folder. Type the title and use Heading 1 for the two section headings; use Normal for body text. Insert page numbers using Insert → Page Number. Check page layout and print preview, then save and verify the filename/location. Q17. Heading styles create consistent formatting and document structure, allowing navigation and an automatically generated table of contents. Page breaks place content reliably on a new page; repeated Enter presses can shift unpredictably when earlier content changes. Q18. Prepare a template and clean spreadsheet (one recipient per row, clear field headings); select Mailings → Start Mail Merge → choose recipients → insert fields → preview several letters → finish/print or send. Check recipient accuracy and sample personalized results. Also confirm permission/privacy, correct attachment/content, and test delivery before sending to everyone."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel","i":"07-spreadsheets-and-excel","x":"07 · Spreadsheets and Excel Q19. Sum formula: =SUM(B2:B5) → 12+18+15+5 = 50 . Average formula: =AVERAGE(B2:B5) → 50/4 = 12.5 . Q20. In D2 , use =C2*$B$1 . $B$1 is absolute so it does not move when copied. In D3 , the formula becomes =C3*$B$1 . For the given row, tax is 250 × 0.16 = 40 . Q21. Sorting only one column breaks the record associations, so names no longer match their original row data. Select the whole table, indicate that it has headers if prompted, then sort by surname. A bar/column chart is suitable for comparing four product sales."},{"u":"practice/ANSWER_KEY.html","g":"Practice","t":"CSC 111 · Worked answer key","h":"08 · Presentations and networks","i":"08-presentations-and-networks","x":"08 · Presentations and networks Q22. Example: (1) title and purpose, high-contrast large title; (2) common risks, short list with one simple icon; (3) prevention steps, numbered sequence; (4) response/reporting and takeaway, one clear action. Keep one main idea per slide, use a consistent theme, cite image/data sources, and rehearse. Other sensible four-part outlines earn credit. Q23. It is a LAN because it covers the office/campus site. A switch connects local devices and forwards traffic within the LAN; a router directs traffic between that LAN and other networks such as the Internet. A benefit is sharing a printer or Internet connection. Q24. Benefits include shared printers/files and faster communication. Risks include unauthorized access and privacy loss; use unique passwords, access permissions, and multi-factor authentication where available. Malware and outages are other risks; keep devices updated, use approved security tools, and maintain separate backups."}];